                                            <td class="timeoriginalestimate"></td>
                                            <td class="timeestimate"></td>
                                            <td class="timespent"></td>
                                            <td class="workratio">&nbsp;
</td>
                                            <td class="subtasks">                </td>
                                            <td class="issuelinks">                </td>
                                            <td class="environment"></td>
                                            <td class="description">      According to the specification &quot;A union is encoded by first writing a long value indicating the zero-based position within the union&quot;. See:<br/>
https://avro.apache.org/docs/1.8.1/spec.html#Unions<br/>
<br/>
However in java implementation index is encoded as an int (which is 32 bits) see: https://avro.apache.org/docs/1.8.1/api/java/org/apache/avro/io/Encoder.html#writeIndex(int)<br/>
<br/>
This causes inconsistencies betewwn diffrent languages, because for instance I can&#39;t deserialize avro serialized with Java using Python, because Python expects long (64 bits) as an index of an union.
  </td>
